+server: a model parks when it is told to, and one answer for "the device has no room now"
+
+0015 let a model's weights leave the device and come back (llama_model_park),
+and parked them each time the context was freed - a switch to measure with.
+This makes parking an ORDER, and gives the refusals of a full device one
+name.
+
+Parking when told. With --context-on-demand and weight buffers that can park:
+- SERVER_TASK_TYPE_PARK, run by the server's own thread between two requests.
+  Told to park: the context is freed, whatever keep its last request asked
+  for - who tells is who manages the device's memory - and the weights leave.
+  Told to come back: they return; the next request creates its context as
+  ever. While a request runs the model is not parked ("busy"). Never a half
+  state: llama_model_park / unpark leave the model as it was when they
+  cannot.
+- a request to a parked model brings the weights back by itself (0015), and
+  the model then STAYS on the device: between two requests nothing parks.
+- POST /park, POST /unpark on llama-server; llama_model_park_size(); the
+  server's meta says can_park; /metrics says weights_parked,
+  weights_park_bytes and the two counts.
+- LLAMA_SERVER_PARK_WEIGHTS stays what it was, a measuring switch.
+
+One answer for no room. ERROR_TYPE_NO_ROOM_ON_DEVICE, HTTP 503, type
+"no_room_on_device", for every way a request finds the device full at that
+moment:
+- parked weights that cannot come back (was 503 unavailable_error, under
+  the message of a context);
+- a context that cannot be created (was 503 unavailable_error);
+- a graph the device refused memory for, llama_process() -2 (was 500
+  server_error "Compute error.", like a fault);
+- and the same refusal when the graph is the CANARY of a context being
+  created: it was answered "context canary failed" and counted in
+  context_canary_failures_total, as if the context had computed something
+  wrong. The canary now says COMMON_CANARY_NO_MEMORY: at a request it is a
+  context that cannot be created, counted nowhere as a failure; at the load
+  it fails the load, where it left the model served unchecked for the life
+  of the process ("the context canary cannot be computed for this model").
+Nothing is wrong with the request or the model in any of them: the same
+request may be sent again.
+
+For the test of a refused return, which a card cannot be made to give on
+demand: GGML_SYCL_PARK_REFUSE=N refuses the next N commits that bring a
+parked buffer back, at the buffer's last piece, raised as the device raises
+its own - so what was committed before it is released as after a real one.
+
+Checked without a device. tools/server/tests: 25 passed (a model that
+cannot park answers 501 and is left alone; the creation refused is
+no_room_on_device). On a CPU stand-in for a device that parks, refuses and
+loses bytes (a dev patch, in no engine): 43 of 43 through /park and /unpark
+- the first vectors bit for bit after each of 10 parks told, a kept context
+freed by the order, the two refusals, 501 for a model that cannot, and orders to park
+beside live requests, each done or refused busy with every request's
+vectors intact; 17 of 17 graph refusals, every one 503 no_room_on_device,
+none counted as a canary that failed. ggml-sycl.cpp compiles with icpx.
+NOT run: any of it on a device.
